# Copy to .env and edit. .env is gitignored - never commit it.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing here is strictly required: cg init prompts for the two secrets if they
# are missing, and every GAME_* value has a default. Setting them here just
# means you are not asked twice.

# --- secrets ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Tailscale pre-auth key, used once to join the instance to your tailnet.
# Generate at https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
#   Reusable:  ON   - a failed build needs a key that has not been spent
#   Ephemeral: OFF  - ephemeral nodes are deleted when they go offline, and this
#                     box goes offline every time the idle watchdog stops it
#   Tag:       optional - a tagged node never expires. Not needed when
#                     TAILSCALE_API_KEY is set: cg init turns key expiry off
#                     itself. The tag must exist in your tailnet ACL policy first.
# This value is written into EC2 user-data, where it stays readable via the
# instance metadata service. Treat a leaked key as compromised and rotate it.
TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY=tskey-auth-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# OPTIONAL. A Tailscale API access token - NOT the auth key above; they are
# different credentials from different pages. With it, cg init prunes the
# offline nodes of earlier boxes, so rebuilds keep the clean hostname instead of
# climbing gamevps-1, -2, -3... - and turns off key expiry on the new box, which
# otherwise drops off the tailnet after ~180 days.
# Create at https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys under
# "API access tokens". Needs write scope on devices.
# Only nodes named exactly <GAME_TS_HOST> or <GAME_TS_HOST>-<number> are ever
# touched; anything else on your tailnet is left alone.
# These tokens expire after 90 days at most. When that happens cg init says so
# explicitly and tells you to regenerate it, rather than silently skipping
# either job - key expiry then shows as a manual step again.
# Not in .env at all: cg check --fix asks for it. TAILSCALE_API_KEY= (empty) means
# go without, and is not asked again.
#TAILSCALE_API_KEY=tskey-api-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# AWS keys are deliberately NOT kept here. They live in ~/.aws/credentials, where
# the aws CLI and every other AWS tool read them; cg check says where yours come
# from. A copy here would be loaded into the environment, where it silently wins
# over ~/.aws - so rotating the key in one place and not the other breaks cg with
# a key that looks fine. And .env is the file you copy between laptops, so the key
# that can create and bill anything is kept out of it.

# Where budget alerts are sent. Comma-separated for several addresses.
# AWS Budgets emails these directly - there is no subscription to confirm.
EMAIL_ALERTS=you@example.com

# --- what gets built ----------------------------------------------------------
# The defaults below are the author's. Region and instance type in particular
# are not free choices: the GPU quota is granted per region, and not every
# region offers every GPU type.

# Region where YOUR GPU quota was approved.
#GAME_REGION=ap-south-2

# 4 vCPU / 16 GB / NVIDIA L4. Check availability before changing:
#   aws ec2 describe-instance-type-offerings --region <region> \
#     --filters Name=instance-type,Values='g*' --query 'InstanceTypeOfferings[].InstanceType'
#GAME_INSTANCE_TYPE=g6.xlarge

# Root volume size. This is the one cost you pay even while stopped, and EBS
# volumes cannot be shrunk later - so start small. Games live on the instance
# store, not here.
#GAME_DISK_GB=50

# Hostname on your tailnet, and the tag used to find the instance again.
#GAME_TS_HOST=gamevps

# Spot instances: ~4x cheaper, but AWS can reclaim them with two minutes' notice.
# Left unset, setup PREFERS SPOT and falls back to on-demand when the spot quota
# cannot cover the instance - so a fresh clone still works. Spot needs its own
# service quota (L-3819A6DF), separate from the on-demand one and 0 by default.
# Set explicitly to override: 1 forces spot, 0 forces on-demand.
#GAME_SPOT=1

# --- the game library ---------------------------------------------------------
# The Steam library, the Proton prefix (which holds save games) and the shader
# cache live on the instance store - local NVMe, free, and the fastest disk
# here - and are mirrored to S3, because the instance store is wiped on every
# stop. `cg init` creates the bucket and remembers its name here; you do not
# need to set this by hand. The name is derived from your account id so it is
# the same every run without being guessable.
#
# Why not an EBS volume, which survives a stop by itself: 160 GB of gp3 bills
# INR ~1,284/month whether or not the box exists, and pins every launch to one
# AZ. The same library in S3 Standard is INR ~310/month and pins nothing. The
# cost is a restore at boot - measured at 235 MB/s on a g6.xlarge, so ~10
# minutes for 140 GB - which runs during the build rather than after it.
#GAME_S3_BUCKET=cg-library-xxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- layer 3: cloud watchdog ---------------------------------------------------
# An AWS Lambda that ends the instance when it looks abandoned, every 5 minutes.
# cg init deploys it and keeps it current; there is nothing to set up.
# docs/cost-guards.md has what it decides and the role it runs on.
#
#   cg watchdog status     schedule and recent decisions
#   cg watchdog check      run it now, as a dry run
#
# Minutes of under 10 MB in+out before it ends the box (at least 10).
#GAME_WATCHDOG_IDLE_MIN=30
# Minutes an instance may spend going down - mirroring its games - before it is
# forced (at least 40: the mirror itself is allowed 30).
#GAME_WATCHDOG_STUCK_MIN=60
# The game archive in S3 is the only thing that bills while no box exists
# (~INR 350/month for 160 GB). The cloud watchdog deletes it after this many
# days with no box launched or running - games then download fresh from Steam,
# and Steam asks you to sign in again. 0 keeps it forever.
# cg status shows the countdown.
#GAME_ARCHIVE_EXPIRY_DAYS=14

# --- notifications ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Push notifications through ntfy (https://ntfy.sh). Off unless this is set: a
# topic name, or a full URL for a self-hosted ntfy. On ntfy.sh anyone who knows a
# topic can read and post to it, so make it long and random - .env is gitignored.
# Sent for idle boxes ended, slow or stuck shutdowns, uploads that fail or hang,
# a box confirmed gone, spot interruptions, the game archive expiring, the cloud
# watchdog failing, and builds - docs/cost-guards.md lists them. Test: cg notify
# Not in .env at all: cg copies it from the cloud watchdog if that already has one
# (so a new laptop keeps notifications on), and cg check --fix asks for it
# otherwise. GAME_NTFY_URL= (empty) turns notifications off.
#GAME_NTFY_URL=

# Budget limit. Alerts fire at 80% of actual spend and 100% of forecast.
#GAME_BUDGET_INR=5000

# --- stream settings ----------------------------------------------------------
# Passed to Moonlight as launch flags. Anything left unset keeps whatever you
# chose in Moonlight's own settings, so this file only overrides what it names.
#
# Bitrate is a CEILING, not a constant: the encoder sends what the picture needs.
# A measured 1280x720 session averaged 2.2 Mbps against a 10 Mbps cap. Egress is
# Mbps x 0.45 = GB/hour, against 100 GB/month free - so at that measured rate
# the allowance is ~100 hours, and lowering the cap mostly costs you quality in
# fast scenes rather than saving money you were spending.
#GAME_RES=1280x720
#GAME_FPS=60
#GAME_BITRATE_KBPS=10000
#GAME_DISPLAY_MODE=borderless      # borderless | fullscreen | windowed
#GAME_VIDEO_CODEC=auto             # auto | HEVC | AV1 | H.264
# Shows live FPS, latency and bitrate over the stream - the honest way to tune
# the settings above instead of guessing.
#GAME_PERF_OVERLAY=0

# Which Sunshine app cg open streams.
#GAME_APP=Desktop

# --- terminal output ----------------------------------------------------------
# On a terminal, cg draws boxed steps, symbols and live progress lines. Off one -
# a pipe, a redirect, the test suites - it prints the plain format, unchanged.
#   auto    styled only on a terminal (default)
#   never   always plain, e.g. if your terminal cannot show emoji
#   always  styled even into a pipe, e.g. `cg init | less -R`
# NO_COLOR=1 (no-color.org) is honoured too.
#CG_COLOR=auto
